  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "brief",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -200,
      "w": 520,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "Story ads, 1080x1920.\nTop row: patients. Bottom row: health professionals.\nEach is a different creative device, not a restyle - run them against each other."
    },
    {
      "id": "brief-ng",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -240,
      "w": 620,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "Patient ads written for a Nigerian audience.\nEvery frame is built to animate: elements are separate, ordered, and id'd - strikes wipe, the route draws, receipt rows stagger, ticks pop, chips settle.\n\"Your assistant\" throughout - never \"AI assistant\".\nFee frames carry a [YOUR FEE] placeholder to fill in."
    },
    {
      "id": "brief-value",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -300,
      "w": 660,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "Value content: gives something instead of asking.\n\nTOP ROW is the current direction - deep navy, the wave motif from src/assets/waves.svg, the real Phoenix wordmark, big editorial type. Each carries one device that animates: the connector line drops, the numeral scales, the question cards deal in, the two panels meet.\n\nBOTTOM ROW is the first pass, kept for comparison - delete when you are done with it.\n\nAny clinical claim needs sign-off from one of your doctors. 'Three questions' and 'Know the difference' carry none.\n[DOCTOR NAME] / [SPECIALTY] are placeholders to fill."
    },
    {
      "id": "brief-facts",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -300,
      "w": 660,
      "page": "page-4",
      "text": "Did you know - a repeatable series. One specialty per post.\n\nEvery one is SCOPE OF PRACTICE: what a specialty covers. That is definitional, not diagnostic, so none of it tells a reader what is wrong with them - which is the line Meta enforces on health ads.\n\nEach maps to a professional type Phoenix actually registers, so the series doubles as an explanation of how broad your marketplace is.\n\nThe well is deep: radiology, nutrition, respiratory therapy, laboratory science and public health are all still unwritten."
    }
  ],
